# `defer.h`

A simple single-header library which adds a defer block to C, similar to Go's defer statement matching proposal [N3199 - Improved `__attribute__((cleanup))` Through `defer`](https://open-std.org/JTC1/SC22/WG14/www/docs/n3199.htm)

## Overview

This library provides syntactic sugar over GCC's cleanup attribute, which
automatically runs a cleanup function when a scope is exited.

Any code placed inside a `defer` block is executed automatically when the
scope is exited, whether by normal fall-through, early return, or a `goto` that skips past it.

Multiple `defer` blocks in the same scope execute in reverse order (LIFO),
matching Go's semantics.

Behind the scenes the macro expands into a GCC nested function attached to a
dummy variable via `__attribute__((cleanup))`, so the cleanup is emitted at
compile time with no runtime registration, no heap allocation, and no library
to link against.

## Supported Platforms

This code uses GCC's nested functions extension, the `cleanup` attribute,
`unused` attribute, and the non-standard `__COUNTER__` macro.

- GCC >= 3.x
- Clang: Unsupported ([#9578 Clang does not support gcc-style nested functions](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/issues/9578))
- MSVC: Unsupported

## Example

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "defer.h"

int main(void)
{
    printf("Hello, World!\n");

    defer { printf("defer #1\n"); }
    defer { printf("defer #2\n"); }
    defer { printf("defer #3\n"); }

    printf("Goodbye, World!\n");

    return 0;
}
```

Output:

```
Hello, World!
Goodbye, World!
defer #3
defer #2
defer #1
```

## Syntax

```ebnf
defer-block:
              defer deferred-block ;

deferred-block:
              compound-statement
```

## FAQ

<details>
<summary>Is this usable <s>in production</s> at all?</summary>

Technically? Yes.
Should you? Hell no.

</details>

<details>
<summary>Why not just use <code>goto cleanup;</code>?</summary>

You absolutely can and should and currently is the idiomatic C pattern.
`defer.h` exists out of pure ~~curiosity~~ stupidity.

</details>

<details>
<summary>Can I use this in C++?</summary>

You can't and shouldn't. C++ already has RAII and destructors, which do the same thing, are
blessed by the standard, and don't require GNU extensions.
</details>

<details>
<summary>Can I <code>return</code> / <code>break</code> inside a defer block?</summary>

The defer body is a nested function, so `return` returns from *the nested
function*, not from your enclosing function. `break` and `continue` won't
reach an outer loop. Treat the block as a small, self-contained cleanup
routine.

</details>

<details>
<summary>What happens if I <code>longjmp</code> past a <code>defer</code>?</summary>

The cleanup does **not** run. `longjmp` unwinds the stack without destroying
variables, so the `cleanup` attribute is never triggered. If you are mixing
`longjmp` and `defer`, you have bigger problems than this library.

</details>

<details>
<summary>Why not support Clang via Objective-C blocks?</summary>

Clang supports blocks (`^(){}`) which are essentially lambdas and could serve
as a replacement for GCC's nested functions. In practice this is cursed because:

- Requires compiling with `-fblocks`.
- Drags in `libBlocksRuntime` as a link-time dependency.
- The syntax is Objective-C bolted onto C.

</details>

